{
 "videoId": "vtY88mBc088",
 "title": "Tactical Paths to 2000 | Smith-Morra, Nimzo, Alapin, London | GM Naroditsky's DYI Speedrun",
 "openingName": "Sicilian Defense",
 "studentSide": "black",
 "voice": "danya-dna",
 "rewrittenAt": "2026-08-25",
 "source": "yt:vtY88mBc088",
 "moves": [
  {
   "ply": 2,
   "t": 47,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [
    "e4",
    "c5"
   ],
   "spoken": "We dive into a Sicilian — c5."
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 48,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [
    "d4"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays d4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 49.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/8/3pP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "cxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "We capture on d4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 51,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/8/3QP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNB1KBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "Qxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "And White recaptures with the queen — a center-game against the Sicilian, an offbeat line we still face at this level.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 69,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/8/3pP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "It doesn't officially have a name — c3 is the Smith-Morra, a knight to f3 usually transposes; this queen recapture is a center-game.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 79.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/8/3QP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNB1KBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "Qxd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "It looks even worse than a center game, because it grants us a lead in development.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 94.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/8/3QP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNB1KBNR w KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "We don't waste time — a knight to c6. The plan is to expand with e6 and d5, though a fianchetto is also possible depending on where White puts the queen."
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 127,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/8/4P3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 2 4",
   "line": [
    "Qd1"
   ],
   "spoken": "White retreats the queen to d1. Our setup depends entirely on where the queen goes.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 132.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/8/3QP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNB1KBNR b KQkq - 6 6",
   "line": [
    "Nd4",
    "Qd2",
    "Nc6",
    "Qd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Even some GMs try this setup with more venom than it appears — titled players go a queen to e3, hiding behind the pawn.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 162.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/8/4P3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 2 4",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "But a queen to d1 isn't testing.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 167,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1ppppp/2n2n2/8/4P3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 3 5",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "The most straightforward option is a knight to f6, starting with flexible moves.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 171.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1ppppp/2n2n2/8/4P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 4 5",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Then the broader choice: g6 and fianchetto, e6 and standard development, or e5 — a viable move; don't fear the d5 weakness.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 209.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/2n2n2/4p3/4P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "I quite like e5.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 214,
   "fen": "r1bqkb1r/pp1p1ppp/2n2n2/1B2p3/4P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "Bb5"
   ],
   "spoken": "e5 fixes the pawn on e4, and with White lagging in development he could end up in serious trouble.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 247.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp1p1ppp/2n2n2/1B2p3/1b2P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 2 7",
   "line": [
    "Bb4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Not a knight to d4, an empty threat — I like a bishop to b4, which develops and threatens a knight capture on e4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 252.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/pp1p1ppp/2B2n2/4p3/1b2P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 7",
   "line": [
    "Bxc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays a bishop capture on c6.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 279,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/p2p1ppp/2p2n2/4p3/1b2P3/2N5/PPP2PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "bxc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "We recapture with the b-pawn — not a bishop capture on c3 (no hurry, and it gives up the dark squares), and not a capture on c5, which allows a queen trade when we have a big lead, the bishop pair, and the prospect of grabbing the center with d5."
  },
  {
   "ply": 15,
   "t": 296.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/p2p1ppp/2p2n2/4p3/1b2P3/2N2P2/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "f3"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays f3 — which favors Black in almost every Sicilian, because it eases grabbing the center with d5.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 16,
   "t": 308.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/2p2n2/3pp3/1b2P3/2N2P2/PPP3PP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 0 9",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "So we grab the center with d5 — no reason to procrastinate; our center is well supported and our king is safe."
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 325,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/2p2n2/3pp1B1/1b2P3/2N2P2/PPP3PP/R2QK1NR b KQkq - 1 9",
   "line": [
    "Bg5"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays a bishop to g5, but his king is in grave danger and we should start winning material. A capture on d4 doesn't win a piece outright, because of the classic a3 technique; but there are other ways to pile on the knight — a queen to a5, or a bishop to a6 to prevent kingside castling.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 18,
   "t": 421.5,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/2p2n2/4p1B1/1b1pP3/2N2P2/PPP3PP/R2QK1NR w KQkq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "d4"
   ],
   "spoken": "We play d4 — and after a3 we play for a long-term advantage rather than grabbing a stray pawn.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 19,
   "t": 425,
   "fen": "r1bqk2r/p4ppp/2p2B2/4p3/1b1pP3/2N2P2/PPP3PP/R2QK1NR b KQkq - 0 10",
   "line": [
    "Bxf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays a bishop capture on f6, making his defense even harder.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 20,
   "t": 431,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/4p3/1b1pP3/2N2P2/PPP3PP/R2QK1NR w KQkq - 0 11",
   "line": [
    "Qxf6"
   ],
   "spoken": "We recapture with the queen, preserving our structure.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 21,
   "t": 440,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/4p3/1b1pP3/2N2P2/PPP1N1PP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 1 11",
   "line": [
    "Ne2"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays a knight to e2 — he loses a piece.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 22,
   "t": 442.5,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/4p3/1b2P3/2p2P2/PPP1N1PP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "dxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "We capture on c3.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 23,
   "t": 444,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/4p3/1b2P3/2P2P2/P1P1N1PP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 12",
   "line": [
    "bxc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "White recaptures. When you win material, the next two or three moves are where the game is won — don't autopilot.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 24,
   "t": 480.5,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/4P3/2P2P2/P1P1N1PP/R2QK2R w KQkq - 1 13",
   "line": [
    "Bc5"
   ],
   "spoken": "So not an autopilot bishop to a5 — a bishop to c5, controlling the dark squares and permanently preventing White from castling, with the pawn fully restricting the e2 knight.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 503.5,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/2P1P3/5P2/P1P1N1PP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 13",
   "line": [
    "c4"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays c4 — it does nothing.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 26,
   "t": 510.5,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/2P1P3/5P2/P1P1N1PP/R2QK2R w KQ - 1 14",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "We castle, since a bishop to a6 is less effective with the pawn on c4."
  },
  {
   "ply": 25,
   "t": 512,
   "fen": "r1b1k2r/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/2P1P3/5P2/P1P1N1PP/R2QK2R b KQkq - 0 13",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 27,
   "t": 513,
   "fen": "r1b2rk1/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/2P1P3/2P2P2/P3N1PP/R2QK2R b KQ - 0 14",
   "line": [
    "O-O",
    "c3"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays c3.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 28,
   "t": 519,
   "fen": "r1br2k1/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/2P1P3/2P2P2/P3N1PP/R2QK2R w KQ - 1 15",
   "line": [
    "Rd8"
   ],
   "spoken": "A rook to d8 looks great — anything is winning, and now we play for mate.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 29,
   "t": 524,
   "fen": "r1br2k1/p4ppp/2p2q2/2b1p3/2P1P3/1QP2P2/P3N1PP/R3K2R b KQ - 2 15",
   "line": [
    "Qb3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Reserve the fancy tactics — you win this through simpler means; playing for flashy tricks is how you blow huge advantages.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 30,
   "t": 568.5,
   "fen": "r1br2k1/p4ppp/2p5/2b1p1q1/2P1P3/1QP2P2/P3N1PP/R3K2R w KQ - 3 16",
   "line": [
    "Qg5"
   ],
   "spoken": "A queen to g5 is awesome, threatening a million things — and I'll note the weak back rank with the d-file open, so I won't blindly grab down there.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 31,
   "t": 622.5,
   "fen": "r1br2k1/p4ppp/2p5/2b1p1q1/2P1P3/1QP2P2/P3N1PP/3RK2R b K - 4 16",
   "line": [
    "Rd1"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays a rook to d1, and we coordinate with a bishop to e6.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 32,
   "t": 624,
   "fen": "r2r2k1/p4ppp/2p1b3/2b1p1q1/2P1P3/1QP2P2/P3N1PP/3RK2R w K - 5 17",
   "line": [
    "Be6"
   ],
   "spoken": "A bishop to e6 completes development — checkmate is a couple of moves away.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 632.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/8/3QP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNB1KBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 2,
   "t": 641.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": "This isn't the Magnus Sicilian, which is a knight to c3 and then d6 — I recommend a knight to c6 instead. The moment g3 is played, think a bishop to h3 automatically: assess which squares a pawn push opened.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 707.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/3PP3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [
    "d4"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 2,
   "t": 709,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 2",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 710,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2",
   "line": [
    "Nc3"
   ],
   "spoken": "Against a knight to c3 my recommendation is a knight to c6.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 711.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 2 3",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 719.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/1Bp5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR b KQkq - 3 3",
   "line": [
    "Bb5"
   ],
   "spoken": "And you'll face a very popular anti-Sicilian, a bishop to b5 — know it backwards and forwards.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 730,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/1Bp5/3nP3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK1NR w KQkq - 4 4",
   "line": [
    "Nd4"
   ],
   "spoken": "A knight to d4 and a knight to f3 — a very trappy line.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 731.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/1Bp5/3nP3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 5 4",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 737.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 2 3",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 739.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4PP2/2N5/PPPP2PP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "f4"
   ],
   "spoken": "f4 is the Grand Prix Attack, but a knight to c6 prevents it...",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 741,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2",
   "line": [],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 742.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 2 3",
   "line": [
    "Nc6"
   ],
   "spoken": "...and prevents White from playing the Magnus Sicilian with d4. If a knight to f3...",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 747,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1ppppp/2n5/2p5/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R b KQkq - 3 3",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 762,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n5/2p1p3/4P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "e5"
   ],
   "spoken": "...a Najdorf player who wants to avoid being move-ordered plays e5 — a separate theoretical line, famously in Kasparov's last classical game against Topalov, Linares 2005.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 805.5,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp1p1ppp/2n5/2p1p3/2B1P3/2N2N2/PPPP1PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Bc4"
   ],
   "spoken": "White plays a bishop to c4, then d3, d6.",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 807,
   "fen": "r1bqkbnr/pp3ppp/2np4/2p1p3/2B1P3/2NP1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQK2R b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "d6",
